  "solution": "<p>The ray-and-arc sketch shows possible third-vertex positions; the sine-rule calculation below tests which positions give valid triangles.</p><p><strong>1.</strong> The sine rule gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>sin</mi><msup><mn>30</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. Let the acute inverse-sine angle be θ. The two possible C-values are θ and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> Two non-congruent, nondegenerate triangles require <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>30</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>: the second triangle otherwise has nonpositive B, while at 90 degrees the two C-values coincide. Equivalently <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> Their B-angles are respectively <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>150</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>30</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>. The latter is already acute in that range. The former is acute exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>sin</mi><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> Combining the strict conditions gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. Answer D. At the lower endpoint one B-angle is right, and at the upper endpoint only one triangle remains.</p>"
